title BUHR-J4r3 — the editor forks a new lane as well as continuing one
prompt Autonomous loop — keep taking agent-doable BUHR laps Lindy-first crux-first, send each increment, STOP at custody gates
think Rung one gave the editor commit (continue an existing lane); the natural pair a file editor needs is open_lane (a new file). commit already refused an unknown lane, so open_lane is the complementary operation — a first revision on a fresh path.
think Added pub open_lane to mantra_editor.rye beside commit: it refuses a path that already carries a leaf (LaneExists) and appends revision 1 otherwise, asserting the new lane opened at exactly one. Both operations are appends to the same append-only Mantra catalog — a fork adds a lane, never a store beside the log.
think Witnessed it in its own app (mantra_editor_fork.rye), matching the one-app-per-rung pattern rather than swelling rung one's selftest. The proof is at the graph level: opening c.txt grows the rendered graph by exactly one lane line (r1, no arrow), lane_count up by one, every existing lane byte-for-byte unchanged; the forked lane then behaves like any lane (a commit chains r1 -> r2); opening an existing lane refuses LaneExists.
obs open_lane and commit are the editor's two appends to one log — "new file" and "edit file" — and because both are appends, the whole editor surface stays inside Mantra's append-only order and immutability laws; there is no rewrite path to guard.
obs Five rounds this session, all green and build-heavy; the editor's core surface (open a lane, commit to a lane, show growth lit) is now complete — a natural place for the next crux to be a design choice (undo-as-read, or the web-read half).
loom witness=buhr_mantra_editor_fork_witness cases=3 editor_rerun=green tame=clean width=rc0
